<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caen is a city on the River <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orne-river">Orne</a> in Normandy, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, who built its castle and, with his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders">Matilda of Flanders</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-etienne-caen">Abbaye aux Hommes</a> and the Abbaye aux Dames in the 1060s; it later housed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exchequer-of-normandy">Exchequer of Normandy</a>. Its limestone was shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for churches and castles, and much of the city was destroyed in the Battle of Normandy in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caen.jpg" alt="Caen" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TTaylor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_Caen_Trinite_c.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
